.docname {Frontend}
.include {docs}

The frontend is one web page with two views side by side. It holds **no model logic**: it shows
what the backend sends and sends back one setting.

| | |
|---|---|
| Language | TypeScript, with Vue 3 for the page |
| 3D | three.js, through TresJS (Vue components that make three.js objects) |
| Build | Vite; it also makes the page installable (a PWA) |
| Server | Caddy, in the container; Vite's own server while developing |

## The page

.mermaid caption:{The one page and what it holds.}
    flowchart TD
        H[HomeView<br/>the page] --> A[AppHeader]
        H --> R[RawView<br/>left]
        H --> T[TwinView<br/>right]
        H --> S[StatusBar]
        R --> SC1[SceneCanvas]
        T --> SC2[SceneCanvas]
        T --> RN[One renderer<br/>per format]

| Piece | What it does |
|---|---|
| `views/HomeView` | The only page. Asks the backend for status every **2 s**, for formats and settings once it answers |
| `AppHeader` | Name, the source ("Offline · map.pcd", "Live · waiting for the robot", or "Backend unreachable"), the run and `seq`, and the **Export** button: it downloads the twin in the format chosen on the right |
| `RawView` | The raw dots, live, on the left |
| `TwinView` | The twin on the right, with one tab per format and the display buttons |
| `StatusBar` | What the last run found, and the wall height input |
| `SceneCanvas` | A 3D canvas with a camera, mouse controls, light and a floor grid. Both views use it |
| `ViewTools` | The buttons for the isometric and the top view |
| `renderers/*` | One file per format; see below |

## Where the data comes from

| Service | Talks to | Gives |
|---|---|---|
| `services/api` | `/api/formats`, `/api/settings`, `/api/status`, `/api/twin/<id>` | Typed answers; an error carries the backend's message |
| `services/rawSocket` | `/ws/raw` | Calls `full`, `delta` and `connection` handlers |
| `services/twinSocket` | `/ws/twin?format=<id>` | The latest twin: text, bytes, or `null` |

Both sockets **retry every second** when the link drops, and stop when the page is left.

.mermaid caption:{From a WebSocket message to pixels.}
    flowchart LR
        WS1["/ws/raw"] --> RS[rawSocket] --> PC[PointCloud] --> RV[RawView]
        WS2["/ws/twin"] --> TS[twinSocket] --> RD[renderer] --> TV[TwinView]
        RD --> SB[scene/twinScene]
        API["/api/status"] --> HV[HomeView] --> ST[StatusBar]

## The raw view

The backend's map frame is "z up"; three.js is "y up". The whole scene is turned once
(`Z_UP_TO_Y_UP`), so every number stays as the backend sent it.

- A **full map** arrives as 12-byte records. The page reads them **in place**, without copying,
  as a `Float32Array` that starts after the 16-byte header (see [Protocol](protocol.qd)).
- A **delta** has 16-byte records: `x y z` and the dot's place. A place below the dot count
  replaces that dot; a place past the end adds one.
- The cloud lives in typed arrays and is **updated in place**. Rebuilding the shapes of an
  800,000-dot cloud for each delta would stall the page.
- Colour goes by height: dark, then cyan, then near white. The ends are the **2nd and 98th
  percentile** of the heights, so a few stray dots do not wash the gradient out.
- The camera moves to the map **only for the first map of a survey**. A reconnect sends the same
  map again; the camera stays where the person left it.

## The twin view

| Format | Renderer | How it draws |
|---|---|---|
| `twin-json` | `TwinJsonScene` | Reads the JSON, builds 3D shapes: a box per wall, an outlined floor per room, a box per object |
| `dxf` | `DxfScene` | Reads the DXF with a small parser; draws lines per layer, with a colour per layer |
| `ply` | `PlyScene` | Reads the binary file with three.js's PLY loader; draws it like the raw cloud |

What `twin-json` shows, from the twin's own fields:

| Part | Drawn as |
|---|---|
| Wall | A box: length from `start` to `end`, depth `thickness`, height `wall_height`, standing on `floor_z`. A label shows its length |
| Room | A faint floor and its outline, in cyan if `closed`, in **orange** if not |
| Object | A box from `bottom_z` to `top_z_seen`, turned by `yaw`. An optional label |
| Wall height | One marker, on the longest wall: all walls share it |

- Each message is the **whole twin**, so each one replaces the shapes of the last. The old shapes
  are freed first.
- Changing the format **closes** the old stream and opens a new one. The camera goes to the new
  twin; a floor plan opens from above.
- Later runs of the same format do **not** move the camera.
- An empty message means "no twin yet": the view shows "Waiting for the first model run".

## The status bar and the one setting

- The bar shows the last report: walls, rooms, objects, time, how many dots were kept, the floor
  tilt and the turn.
- **Points kept** is `after_outliers / points_in`. The real room: 186,427 / 192,149 = **97.0%**.
- The wall height input waits **half a second** after the last key, then sends one `PUT`. Each `PUT`
  makes the backend run the model again; this stops one run per key.
- If the backend refuses the value, the message appears beside the input.

## Types

| File | How it is made |
|---|---|
| `types/twin.ts` | **Generated** from `backend/schema/twin.schema.json` with `npm run generate-types` |
| `services/api.ts` | Written by hand: formats, settings, status |

## Building and serving

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `npm run dev` | Vite's server with hot reload; sends `/api` and `/ws` to `localhost:9002` (or `VCORTEX_BACKEND`) |
| `npm run build` | Type check, then build into `dist/` |
| `npm run type-check` | `vue-tsc`, a type checker that also reads `.vue` files |
| `npm run lint` | oxlint, then ESLint |
| `npm run format` | Prettier |
| `npm run generate-types` | Rebuild `types/twin.ts` |

**In the container**, Caddy serves the built page on port 8080 and passes `/api` and `/ws` to the
backend. So the browser sees **one address**, and there is nothing to configure for cross-site
requests.

| Path | Cache |
|---|---|
| `/assets/*` | One year. Vite puts a hash in each name, so a changed file has a new name |
| Everything else (`index.html`, the service worker, the manifest) | Checked on each load, or a new release would never reach the browser |

### The installable app (PWA)

- The page can be installed on a phone or a laptop. It updates itself.
- Its files are kept for offline start. `/api` and `/ws` are **never** cached: they are live data.
- The icons are made from `public/logo.svg`.

## Gaps found

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| `frontend/README.md` is still the text of the Vue project template; it says nothing about vCortex | `frontend/README.md` |
| The HTTP types are written by hand, so a change in the backend's status answer is not caught by the type checker | `services/api.ts` |
| The page sets only the wall height; the other settings need a `PUT` by hand | `StatusBar.vue` |
